engine: model:blind-entailer
output: |
  body: |
    The statement reads: for every natural number k, if k is not 0, then the set of natural numbers p such that p is prime and p is congruent to 1 modulo k is infinite. Here p is congruent to 1 modulo k when p and 1 leave the same remainder on division by k; modulo 1 every number is congruent to 1. [C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one, L:term/congruent-modulo-k]

    The logic runs in six stages. Stage 1 is a reduction: to show the set is infinite, it is enough to produce, for each bound n, one such prime p with n < p. Its key lemma is `Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one`: for every natural number k and every natural number n, if k is not 0, there is a prime p with n < p and p congruent to 1 modulo k. The obvious route, multiplying the primes already found and adding one, fails here, because nothing forces its prime factors into the class 1 modulo k. The file instead evaluates the k-th cyclotomic polynomial, the monic polynomial with integer coefficients whose complex roots are exactly the primitive k-th roots of unity, and its prime factors are forced into that class. [C:part/one-beyond-every-bound, C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, K:concept/cyclotomic-polynomial]

    Stage 2 handles the modulus one. Since k is not 0, either k = 1 or k > 1. For k = 1 the congruence asks nothing, so any prime at least n + 1 serves, and the infinitude of primes supplies one. The case is split off because the lower bound used next is stated only for k > 1. [C:part/the-modulus-one, C:step/case-k-one, C:reliance/infinite-primes, N:decision/split-off-k-one]

    Stage 3 builds the candidate prime. For k > 1 put b = k times n factorial, where n factorial is the product 1 times 2 times ... times n; then b is at least 2. The key lemma is a lower bound: for k > 1 and a natural number q other than 1, q - 1 is less than the absolute value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial at q. So that value at b exceeds b - 1, which is at least 1, and it has a least prime factor p, the smallest divisor greater than 1, which is always prime. Because p divides the value, the image of b in the integers modulo p (the field of remainders on division by p) is a root of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial there. One reading: the proof can name a prime at all only because the value exceeds 1. [C:part/the-candidate-prime, C:step/value-exceeds-one, C:step/least-prime-factor, C:step/root-mod-p, L:term/n-factorial, L:term/least-prime-factor, L:term/the-integers-modulo-p, C:reliance/cyclotomic-value-bound, C:reliance/least-factor-prime, C:reliance/cyclotomic-reduction, C:reliance/zero-mod-p, P:reading/the-value-has-a-prime-factor]

    Stage 4 puts the candidate past the bound. A root of a cyclotomic polynomial modulo p is coprime to p, so p does not divide b. Every number from 1 to n divides n factorial and so divides b; a prime p at most n would divide b, so n < p. The reading: the factor n factorial is what makes the prime large. [C:part/past-the-bound, C:step/p-not-dividing-b, C:step/p-above-n, C:reliance/root-coprime, C:reliance/divides-factorial, P:reading/factorial-pushes-past-n]

    Stage 5 shows the order of b is exactly k. The multiplicative order of an element is the least positive power of it that equals 1. By the little theorem of Fermat, in the form of orders, a nonzero element modulo p has order dividing p - 1. Since k divides b and p does not, k is nonzero modulo p, so a root of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial there is a primitive k-th root of unity, an element whose k-th power is 1 and no smaller positive power is, so its order is k. Hence k divides p - 1, which is p congruent to 1 modulo k. Two readings: the factor k in b keeps k nonzero modulo p, and the congruence comes from the order, not from any search. [C:part/order-exactly-k, C:step/order-is-k, K:concept/multiplicative-order, K:concept/little-theorem-of-fermat, K:concept/primitive-root-of-unity, C:reliance/fermat-order, C:reliance/cyclotomic-root-primitive, C:reliance/congruence-as-divisibility, P:reading/the-factor-k-keeps-k-nonzero, P:reading/order-forces-the-congruence]

    Stage 6 returns to infinitely many. A property holds frequently at infinity when it holds for arbitrarily large p. The lemma `Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one` unfolds this into "for every n some p at least n has the property", taking that p from the existence lemma and weakening n < p to n at most p; on the natural numbers, holding frequently at infinity is the same as holding on an infinite set. We read this stage as only a change of language. [C:part/back-to-infinitely-many, C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one, L:term/frequently-at-infinity, C:reliance/frequently-unfolded, C:reliance/frequently-infinite, P:reading/only-a-change-of-language]

    What the proof delivers is more than the statement keeps. For each k other than 0 and each bound n it names one prime congruent to 1 modulo k above n; for k = 1, any prime at least n + 1. For k > 1 we read that the argument uses only that p is a prime dividing the value, so every prime factor of the absolute value of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial at k times n factorial is above n and congruent to 1 modulo k, and the least one lies between the bounds: strictly greater than n and at most that value. This reading needs two changes the file does not make: an arbitrary prime factor in place of the least, and the fact that the least prime factor is at most the number. The statement keeps only that the set is infinite. A smaller point, such as k times the least common multiple of 1 to n, may give a smaller upper end. [P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers, N:decision/least-prime-factor, N:decision/evaluate-at-k-times-n-factorial]

    A worked example, k = 3 and n = 2: b = 3 times 2 factorial = 6, and the third cyclotomic polynomial is x squared plus x plus 1, with value 43 at 6. As 43 is prime, p = 43; 43 > 2, and 43 - 1 = 42 = 3 times 14, so 43 is congruent to 1 modulo 3; and 6 cubed = 216 = 5 times 43 plus 1, so 6 has order exactly 3 modulo 43. [P:case/three-and-two]

    On checking: the file's text holds no `sorry` and declares no axiom of its own. It was read here as text, by rules, and nothing was built or run, so whether Lean accepts it is not known here. [P:claim/checking, P:assumption/read-as-text]
